212	// ── DEVICE LAYER ──────────────────────────────────────
213	
214	{ch:'device',type:'mc',
215	 q:'What does <code>_register(path, fn)</code> store, and when is it called?',
216	 opts:[
217	   {t:'Stores <code>fn</code> in <code>self._handlers[path.upper()]</code>; called inside <code>_setup_handlers()</code>, which is invoked by <code>super().__init__()</code>.',correct:true,why:'Correct. _register uppercases the path and inserts the handler. _setup_handlers() is called by super().__init__(), so by the time __init__ returns all handlers are registered.'},
218	   {t:'Stores <code>fn</code> in <code>self._handlers[path]</code> (case-preserved); called by the subclass directly in its own <code>__init__</code>.',correct:false,why:'Wrong on two counts: _register() normalizes the key to uppercase, and it should be called inside _setup_handlers(), not scattered through __init__.'},
219	   {t:'Stores <code>fn</code> in <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY</code>; called after <code>super().__init__()</code> once the instance is fully constructed.',correct:false,why:'BLOCK_REGISTRY is a canvas.py dict of block classes, not a handler map. _register() stores into self._handlers, not any module-level registry.'},
220	   {t:'Stores a string path in <code>self._commands</code>; <code>fn</code> is looked up at dispatch time from the class <code>__dict__</code>.',correct:false,why:'_register() stores the callable directly under the normalized path in self._handlers — no late lookup. self._commands is not a real attribute in BaseDevice.'},
221	 ]},
222	
223	{ch:'device',type:'wr',
224	 q:'Describe the handler contract for write vs query commands. What must each type return, and what happens if a write handler returns a non-empty string?',
225	 concepts:[
226	   {label:'writes return ""',terms:['""','empty string','return ""']},
227	   {label:'queries return str value',terms:['query','str(','return f','return "']},
228	   {label:'non-empty from write echoed to caller',terms:['echo','caller','response','non-empty','append']},
229	   {label:'ScpiError for invalid input',terms:['scpierror','scpi_error','ScpiError','raise']},
230	 ],
231	 model:'Write handlers must return "" (empty string). The return value is collected by process() — non-empty strings, regardless of command type, are appended to the response list and returned to the caller as if they were query results. Query handlers return the value as a string (e.g. f"{self.state.voltage:.4f}"). For out-of-range or invalid input, raise ScpiError(code, message) — BaseDevice._dispatch() catches it and queues it for SYSTEM:ERROR?. Never raise plain ValueError.',
232	},
233	
234	{ch:'device',type:'wr',
235	 q:'Why must <code>self.state = MyState()</code> come BEFORE <code>super().__init__()</code>? Trace exactly what breaks if the order is reversed.',
236	 concepts:[
237	   {label:'super().__init__ calls _setup_handlers',terms:['_setup_handlers','setup_handler']},
238	   {label:'handlers reference self.state',terms:['self.state','state']},
239	   {label:'crash on first dispatch not __init__',terms:['first','dispatch','process','attributeerror','attribute']},
240	 ],
241	 model:'super().__init__() calls _setup_handlers(), which registers handler methods like _volt() that close over self. Those methods are NOT called at registration time — they are just stored. So __init__ itself won\'t crash. The crash happens later, on the first process("VOLT?") call: _dispatch() calls _volt(), which reads self.state — which doesn\'t exist yet — raising AttributeError. Rule: all instance variables must be set before super().__init__().',
242	},
243	
244	{ch:'device',type:'mc',
245	 q:'A device needs to support both <code>VOLT</code> and <code>SOURCE:VOLTAGE</code> routed to the same handler. Which approach is correct?',
246	 opts:[
247	   {t:'Call <code>self._register("VOLT", self._volt)</code> and <code>self._register("SOURCE:VOLTAGE", self._volt)</code> — register the same callable under both paths.',correct:true,why:'Correct. _register() can map multiple paths to the same function. There is no de-duplication check; both entries just point to self._volt.'},
248	   {t:'Register only the canonical long-form path and rely on SCPI abbreviation expansion inside _dispatch() to match "VOLT".',correct:false,why:'BaseDevice._dispatch() does a direct dict lookup — it does not expand SCPI short-forms. Both paths must be registered explicitly.'},
249	   {t:'Use <code>self._register_alias("SOURCE:VOLTAGE", "VOLT")</code> only — _register_alias() handles multi-path routing natively.',correct:false,why:'_register_alias() stores an alias, but the primary ("VOLT") must also be registered. Using only _register_alias without a primary registration means the primary path isn\'t handled.'},
250	   {t:'Override <code>_normalize_path()</code> to map both strings to a single canonical form before _dispatch() looks them up.',correct:false,why:'BaseDevice does not have a _normalize_path() hook that subclasses are expected to override. Multi-path support is done via multiple _register() calls.'},
251	 ]},
252	
253	{ch:'device',type:'wr',
254	 q:'What is <code>_do_reset()</code> for? When is it called? Write the body of <code>_do_reset()</code> for a device whose only state is <code>self.state = MyState()</code>.',
255	 concepts:[
256	   {label:'called on *RST or reset()',terms:['*rst','rst','reset()','reset']},
257	   {label:'restore factory defaults',terms:['default','factory','restore','initial']},
258	   {label:'body: self.state = MyState()',terms:['mystate()','MyState','self.state =']},
259	 ],
260	 model:'_do_reset() is called by BaseDevice.reset(), which is triggered by *RST (the IEEE 488.2 reset command) or right-click → "Reset device". It restores the device to factory defaults. For a device with a single state dataclass, the correct body is: def _do_reset(self) -> None: self.state = MyState()',
261	},
262	
263	{ch:'device',type:'wr',
264	 q:`Read this snippet from <code>silmulator/devices/power_supply.py</code>:
265	
266	<pre class="snippet"><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
267	    self.channels: Dict[int, ChannelState] = {
268	        i: ChannelState() <span class="kw">for</span> i <span class="kw">in</span> range(<span class="num">1</span>, self.NUM_CHANNELS+<span class="num">1</span>)
269	    }
270	    self.selected_channel: int = <span class="num">1</span>
271	    <span class="kw">super</span>().__init__()</pre>
272	
273	Why is the dict comprehension on lines 2-4 placed before super().__init__(), and what breaks if it's moved after?`,
274	 concepts:[
275	   {label:'super calls _setup_handlers',terms:['_setup_handlers','setup_handler']},
276	   {label:'handlers close over self.channels',terms:['self.channels','channels']},
277	   {label:'attributeerror on first dispatch',terms:['attributeerror','attribute error','dispatch','process']},
278	 ],
279	 model:'The dict comprehension builds self.channels before super().__init__() is called. super().__init__() calls _setup_handlers(), which registers handler methods (e.g. _set_voltage) that access self.channels. If self.channels were created after super(), those handlers would be registered before self.channels exists. On the first SCPI dispatch, a handler like self._set_voltage() would try to read self.channels and raise AttributeError. Same rule applies to selected_channel.',
280	},
281	
282	// ── BLOCK LAYER ──────────────────────────────────────
283	
284	{ch:'block',type:'mc',
285	 q:'What arguments does <code>add_port(id, label, x, y)</code> accept, and what do <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> represent?',
286	 opts:[
287	   {t:'<code>id</code>: string key; <code>label</code>: displayed text; <code>x, y</code>: fractions of BLOCK_WIDTH and BLOCK_HEIGHT (0.0–1.0).',correct:true,why:'Correct. BaseBlock.add_port() calls setPos(BLOCK_WIDTH * x, BLOCK_HEIGHT * y) on the ConnectionPort, so 0.0 is the top/left edge and 1.0 is the bottom/right edge.'},
288	   {t:'<code>id</code>: string key; <code>label</code>: displayed text; <code>x, y</code>: pixel offsets from the block\'s top-left corner.',correct:false,why:'x and y are fractions, not pixels. BaseBlock.add_port() multiplies them by BLOCK_WIDTH and BLOCK_HEIGHT to get pixel positions.'},
289	   {t:'<code>id</code>: integer index; <code>label</code>: displayed text; <code>x, y</code>: scene coordinates.',correct:false,why:'port id is a string, and x/y are fractional (relative to block size), not scene coordinates. Scene coordinates are computed from the block\'s position plus the port offset.'},
290	   {t:'<code>id</code>: string key; <code>label</code>: displayed text; <code>x, y</code>: fractions where x=0.5 is always centered horizontally.',correct:false,why:'x=0.5 IS centered horizontally, but x is not "always" centered — you choose any fraction from 0.0 to 1.0. The description of what x/y represent is also wrong (they are fractions of block dimensions, which is correct for x=0.5 being the center).'},
291	 ]},
292	
293	{ch:'block',type:'wr',
294	 q:'What are the four overrideable hooks in <code>BaseBlock</code>? Which one is required? When does each get called?',
